<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter6_what-is-iteration">
    <title>What Iteration Means</title>

    <p>
        Many everyday activities involve repeating an action. For example,
        imagine packing groceries into a reusable grocery bag. You place one
        item into the bag, check whether another item fits safely, and continue
        until no more items fit safely.
    </p>

    <p>
        In this situation, the number of repetitions is not known in advance.
        The process is controlled by a condition: continue <q>until</q> the bag
        is full enough to stop packing. This is the kind of repetition commonly
        expressed with a <c>while</c> statement.
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_iteration_until_condition"
             language="cpp">
        <code><![CDATA[
while (another_item_fits_safely) {
    place_one_item_in_bag();
}
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        Now imagine counting 100 items in a shipment. You know that the count
        should begin at 1, increase by 1 each time, and stop after reaching 100.
        This is the kind of repetition commonly expressed with a <c>for</c>
        statement.
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_iteration_known_count"
             language="cpp">
        <code><![CDATA[
for (int count = 1; count <= 100; ++count) {
    std::cout << count << '\n';
}
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        Words in a problem statement can provide useful clues about the kind of
        repetition that is needed.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table_iteration_vocabulary">
        <title>Vocabulary that describes repetition</title>
        <tabular>
            <row>
                <cell>Words or phrases</cell>
                <cell>What they suggest</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><q>until</q> or <q>as long as</q></cell>
                <cell>Continue while a condition is true.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><q>exactly 100 times</q></cell>
                <cell>Repeat a known number of times.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><q>from 1 through 100</q></cell>
                <cell>Count through a known range.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><q>for each item</q></cell>
                <cell>Process every value in a collection.</cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
        These words are clues, not automatic rules. Before choosing a loop, ask
        what action must be repeated and what event should end the repetition.
        This type of repetition is called <term>iteration</term>.
    </p>
</section>
